** 1. Knowledge Check - Scrum roles and accountability
*** Why does Scrum emphasise small, self-managing teams with clear roles rather than hierarchies or sub-teams?

As scrum is an iterative framework for development, it allows for continuous feedback and flexibility, this approach emphasises small, self-managing teams as they are able to quickly respond to new stimuli. This approach is formed from the notion of requirement volatility, that stakeholders will change requirements as the project evolves.

Self managing teams do not require a manager to make the final decision, deciding themselves on how to best approach and do not have to be slowed by outside bureaucracy.

Hierarchies and sub-teams delay the flow of information to and from. Information has to flow through many levels to reach the relevant party/individuals. This goes against the scrums goal of fast feedback and change. 


** Scrum Role - Scrum Master

*** What does accountability mean for a Scrum Master?

Protecting the purpose of the scrum and helping people understand why the framework exists. Ensures all understand Scrum theory and practice, both within and outside of the team. Fostering the culture of the team, especially around continuous improvement. Leading the retrospection for the team and encouraging discussion about the processes and collaboratively identify improvements. 

Servant leader - advocates for the team and ensures all needs are met. Remove external impediments that would impact the smooth development process. Understanding the need for clear and concise Product Backlog items. 

Guiding hand that steers the boat through unknown waters to lead them to new lands. 


** 3. AI Conversation Reflection

*** Chat 1 - Purpose of a sprint pilot 

Insight - the sprint pilot has lower stakes involved in the whole process, this allows for teams to be more experimentive in their approach rather than relying on old and trusted method's. 

Despite the lower stakes, it can still be hard for people to experiment with new methods. Ice-breakers and the such can work for getting people out of their comfort room but can be re-hearsed. So changing it to something out of left field like karaoke, which could be very uncomfortable and will be completely new process for some could instill a sense of exploration and broaden people's horizon.

*** Chat 2 - Sprint planning - Roleplay - one planning practice that i will apply to my team.
"Definition of Done" 
Shared set of criteria determined among the group/stakeholders of when a product or backlog item is done or completed. 

Shared understanding of what the final product will look like to all parties.

Start a collaborative chat, highlighting the key parts and forming them into actionable statements. Stops potential confusion of what needs to be delivered and when. 

*** Inspect and adapt

Checking progress regularly to see if it's heading to the sprint goal and adjusting accordingly if things are not working or new info pops up.


