On negotiating responsibilities, I declared myself the scrum master without much input from my teammates and failed to have an appropriate discussion beforehand. In my defence, one teammate was away and the other did not know too much about the scrum roles.

When I conducted my retrospection of the day, I felt like it was somewhat needed to keep the operation going smoothly and to derail into unneeded conversation. However, I felt guilty in not taking into consideration the input that they would have given.

What worked well was that we had nearly fully completed the assigned tasks of the week, unlike others where external meetings were needed to catch up.
What did not go well was that as one teammate was away for the first part of the class, they were unaware of the discussion of the roles and the executive decision that I made. My action also makes it look like I do not value their opinions.

I took the role upon myself to keep the flow of discussion moving forwad, proritising speed and actual fruitful discussion that could have come from it. They were two factors that affected my decision. The absent teammate and the lack of team role knowledge that my other teammate displayed.

The scrum guide tells that a scrum master serves the team. It does not command it. Upon taking the role without agreement, I acted against the principle.

For the next session, a quick explanation of the team-roles and what each entails would go a long way for a fair and democratic process. 
